  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1110 AM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nThe leading edge of precipitation pushes across into the central\nmountains and highlands along the Utah/Nevada border by late\nafternoon/early evening. The bulk of the moisture moves in\novernight and tomorrow morning. With the storm splitting as it\nsweeps through, highest amounts will be across the Wood River\nValley and surrounding mountains (Sawtooths, Boulders, and\nPioneers), and also extending from the South Hills/Albion\nMountains through the Bear River Range. Most areas will see\nunder 0.20\" or less especially where any downslope occurs or \nlocations closer to the Montana border. For the Wood River\nValley area, the latest forecast runs at 0.25-0.55\" with high\nend of the range falling above pass level in the surrounding\nmountains. This equates to around 1-3\" up through 7000-9000ft,\nlocally higher in the Boulders and Pioneers. Across the\nhighlands south of the Snake Plain and Magic Valley, look for\n0.20-0.40\" at mid and upper slopes. Again, 1-3\" of snow is\npossible above 7000ft. Other mountain ranges should end up\nanywhere from a Trace to 2-3\" at most. As we have been saying\nmost of this week, we have boosted the WPC forecast by\n0.05-0.10\" as it seems a bit low considering all of the\nensemble and AI versions of various models as well as the RRFS\nand HRRR. Some of these seem a bit ambitious with at least\ndouble the amount we are forecasting. With how quickly this low\nis moving through and splitting in the process, these numbers\nsee a bit out of reach. That said, we may still be a bit low as\nthere are some hints we might see a few embedded heavier bands\nof precipitation, as well as a few thunderstorms. The cold front\nwill be through here by lunchtime Saturday. Highs tomorrow will\nin the 30s and 40s areawide. Breezy southerly winds ahead of the\nfront will turn westerly behind it. Those southerly winds are\nexpected to cut down precipitation totals due to downslope from\nBurley through Rexburg along the benches. \n\nSunday will be dry with temperatures rebounding 5-10 degrees,\nbut still only in the mid 40s to mid 50s. The next storm slowly\nmoves onshore Monday, with any real chance of rain and snow\nacross the central mountains. That particular storm, again will\nbe splitting and quickly moving through here by Tuesday night.\nThe next low will follow on its heals, but again trying to\nsplit. One of the more certain trends is toward much cooler\ntemperatures. This MAY mean seeing some snow falling (not\nnecessarily accumulating except on non-road surfaces) at lower\nelevations if things time out correctly. We do not have much\nconfidence with how much falls with any particular wave of\nmoisture at the moment.",
